<BaseLayout title="Indirect object pronouns in Spanish | LearnNest" description="Spanish indirect object pronouns — me, te, le, nos, os, les — explained simply, with examples and common mistakes to avoid." path="/guides/indirect-object-pronouns-spanish" faqs={faqs}>
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › Indirect object pronouns in Spanish</p>
  <h1>Indirect object pronouns in Spanish</h1>
  <p class="lede">Indirect object pronouns in Spanish are small words — me, te, le, nos, os, les — that tell you who receives something or who something is done for. They look small, but they appear in almost every Spanish conversation, so learning them is one of the best investments a beginner can make.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>What is an indirect object?</h2>
  <p>An indirect object is the person who receives something, or for whom something is done. In “I give the book to Maria”, “the book” is the direct object (what is given) and “Maria” is the indirect object (who receives it). In Spanish, the indirect object is usually shown twice: once with a pronoun, and once with “a + person”.</p>
  <p>For example: “Le doy el libro a María” — literally “I give the book to Maria”, with “le” (to her) before the verb and “a María” after it. English does not need both parts, but Spanish usually does, and it sounds wrong without the pronoun.</p>
  <h2>The six indirect object pronouns</h2><table class="word-table">
      <tr><th>Pronoun</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      <tr><td>me</td><td>to me / for me</td><td>Me das el libro. — You give me the book.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>te</td><td>to you / for you (informal)</td><td>Te escribo una carta. — I write you a letter.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>le</td><td>to him / to her / to you (formal)</td><td>Le doy el libro a María. — I give the book to Maria.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>nos</td><td>to us / for us</td><td>Nos envían un correo. — They send us an email.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>os</td><td>to you / for you (plural, Spain)</td><td>Os digo la verdad. — I tell you the truth.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>les</td><td>to them / to you (plural)</td><td>Les doy las llaves. — I give them the keys.</td></tr>
    </table>
  <h2>Where do the pronouns go?</h2>
  <p>With a conjugated verb, the pronoun goes before the verb: “Te llamo mañana” (I will call you tomorrow), “Nos gusta el café” (We like coffee — literally, “coffee is pleasing to us”).</p>
  <p>With an infinitive or a gerund, the pronoun can attach to the end: “Quiero llamarte” (I want to call you) or “Estoy llamándote” (I am calling you). Both positions are correct; Spanish speakers use whichever sounds natural in the sentence.</p>
  <h2>The special case: le becomes se</h2>
  <p>When an indirect object pronoun (le or les) comes before a direct object pronoun (lo, la, los, las), the indirect pronoun changes to “se”. So “I give it to him” is not “Le lo doy” but “Se lo doy”. This happens in almost every conversation — “Se lo digo” (I tell him), “Se la envío” (I send it to her) — so learn it as a fixed pattern, not as a rule to work out each time.</p>
  <p>The same change happens with “les”: “Se los doy” (I give them to them). If this feels confusing, remember the simple version: whenever le or les meets lo, la, los or las, le becomes se.</p>
  <h2>Gustar and other verbs that need these pronouns</h2>
  <p>Some of the most useful Spanish verbs work “backwards” compared with English, and they need indirect object pronouns. “Me gusta el café” means “I like coffee”, but literally it is “Coffee is pleasing to me”. The same pattern works for me encanta (I love it), me interesa (it interests me), me duele (it hurts me) and me parece (it seems to me).</p>
  <p>Learn these verbs with their pronoun from the start — “me gusta”, “te gusta”, “le gusta” — and they will feel natural much sooner.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Forgetting the pronoun and saying only “Doy el libro a María” — Spanish needs “Le doy el libro a María”.</li>
    <li>Saying “le lo doy” instead of “se lo doy” — le always becomes se before lo, la, los or las.</li>
    <li>Confusing le (to him/her) with lo (him/it as a direct object) — “Le veo” and “Lo veo” mean different things.</li>
    <li>Putting the pronoun after a conjugated verb — it goes before the verb, not after.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn the six pronouns with one example each, and say them aloud until they are automatic.</li>
    <li>Practise with gustar verbs first — me gusta, te gusta, le gusta — because you will use them every day.</li>
    <li>When you hear “se lo” or “se la” in Spanish, notice it: that is the le-to-se rule in action.</li>
    <li>Write five sentences about giving things to people in your life, using a different pronoun each time.</li>
  </ul>

