  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>What is the difference between le and lo in Spanish?</summary><p>Le is an indirect object pronoun — it shows who receives something (to him, to her). Lo is a direct object pronoun — it shows what is acted on (him, it). Compare “Le doy el libro” (I give the book to him) with “Lo veo” (I see him). They look similar but do different jobs.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Why do Spanish speakers say “se lo” instead of “le lo”?</summary><p>Because when le or les comes before lo, la, los or las, it changes to se. It is a sound rule: Spanish avoids the “le lo” combination. So “I give it to him” is always “Se lo doy”, never “Le lo doy”.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Where does the indirect object pronoun go in a sentence?</summary><p>Before a conjugated verb: “Te llamo mañana.” With an infinitive or gerund, it can attach to the end: “Quiero llamarte.” Both are correct — the meaning is the same.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Do I really need to use these pronouns?</summary><p>Yes — they appear in almost every Spanish conversation, especially with verbs like gustar (to like), dar (to give), decir (to say) and enviar (to send). Without them, your Spanish will sound incomplete to native speakers.</p></details>
